														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1392998">
									

<blockquote>
<p>An infestation typically ends with the deaths of the parents and other caretakers as they neglect their own well-being in order to attend to SCP-1076, eventually succumbing to starvation or disease.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What happens then? Does the SCP simply move onto more victims? Does it grow into the adult phase of its life-cycle? Maybe it eats the corpses of the caretakers?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon investigating, these authorities become obsessed with the welfare of SCP-1076 and subsequently ignore the children.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Seems like this could cause an infestation to rapidly spiral into something needing a lot of cover up: when the civilians are no longer able to care for the SCP the social worker takes it to an orphanage. The adults at the orphanage all obsess over the SCP, leaving all the other orphans unfed and unsupervised. Other orphans all get into a lot of trouble, drawing attention.</p>
<p>Alternatively: social worker #1 investigates, becomes obsessed with SCP, drops all other cases due to obsession. Social worker #2 wonders what's up with that, investigates, becomes obsessed; lather, rinse, repeat. Soon the entire social services office of the city has ceased to function, drawing attention.</p>

							</div>

								
					
